#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/path.z", which is the one
place they are written, each with its doc comment. This page is the prose.

#~"path" handles file system paths as the operating system the program is
built for spells them, as Go's #~"path/filepath" does. On Windows #~"\\"
is the separator and #~"/" is read as one too, and a path may begin with a
drive (#~"C:\\"), a share (#~"\\\\server\\share\\") or the verbatim
prefix (#~"\\\\?\\"); elsewhere #~"/" is the only separator and the only
root. #~"join" and #~"clean" write the native separator, so a path a
program composes is one the system and its users read as their own. Every
function is pure text: none asks the file system. The system the build is
for is a compile-time answer (#~"z.build.os"), so a cross build for
Windows composes Windows paths.


#--: Index

#ul:
  [#*: #~"separator" -- #~"function out String": #~"\\" on Windows, #~"/" elsewhere]
  [#*: #~"listSeparator" -- #~"function out String": what separates a list of paths, as #~"PATH" spells it: #~";" on Windows, #~":" elsewhere]
  [#*: #~"isSeparator" -- #~"function {b: u8} out bool"]
  [#*: #~"rootLength" / #~"root" -- the root's bytes, and the root with native separators]
  [#*: #~"isAbsolute" -- #~"function {p: StringView} out bool"]
  [#*: #~"clean" -- #~"function {p: StringView} out String"]
  [#*: #~"join" -- #~"function {a: StringView b: StringView} out String"]
  [#*: #~"dirname" / #~"baseName" -- #~"function {p: StringView} out String"]
  [#*: #~"toSlash" -- #~"function {p: StringView} out String": #~"p" with #~"/" for each separator -- on Windows each #~"\\" becomes #~"/", elsewhere #~"p" as written (Go's #~"filepath.ToSlash"). For text spelled with #~"/" whatever the system: a URI, a key, a golden.]


#--: Roots and absolute paths

A root is what a path is anchored to. On POSIX it is #~"/". On Windows it
is a drive with its separator (#~"C:\\"), a share
(#~"\\\\server\\share\\"), a verbatim or device path
(#~"\\\\?\\C:\\", #~"\\\\?\\UNC\\server\\share\\"), a drive alone
(#~"C:", that drive's current directory) or a separator alone (#~"\\",
the current drive's root). #~"isAbsolute" is true when a path names the
same place from any working directory: on Windows that rules out
#~"C:x" and #~"\\x", which depend on the current drive and its
directory.


#--: Clean, join, dirname and baseName

#~"clean" answers the shortest path naming the same place by text alone:
one native separator between components, #~"." components dropped, each
#~".." taking the component before it (never climbing past a root), no
trailing separator, and #~"." for an empty result.

#~"join" puts one native separator between its two parts and cleans the
result; an absolute second part stands alone, and an empty part
contributes nothing.

#~"dirname" is the path without its last component, cleaned: the root for
a path directly in it, #~"." for a relative path of one component.
#~"baseName" is the last component, trailing separators ignored, and
#~"\"\"" when there is none (an empty path, or a root alone).

#code language=zerolang: """
  p: path.join a: "build" b: "out/zc"
  print p                  # build/out/zc, or build\out\zc on Windows
  print (path.dirname p: p.stringView)   # build/out
  print (path.baseName p: p.stringView)  # zc
"""
